324:### 12.2 Experiment runner: when it may touch code
325-
326-**Input.** A `PLAN.md`, written and hashed before the runner starts. It states:
327-
328-- the mechanism under test  
329-- the metric and threshold  
330-- seeds or trials  
331-- the maximum number of attempts  
332-- the control or null condition, if any  
333-- the exact command  
334-- the output format
335-
336-**May:**
337-
338-- write harness code, environment setup, instrumentation, and data plumbing  
339-- fix defects so the code does what `PLAN.md` says
340-
341-**Must not:**
342-
343-- change the mechanism under test, the metric, the threshold, the seed or trial count, the attempt cap, or the pass rule  
344-- choose which runs to report  
345-- edit the brief, rubric, decision, or claim records  
346-- write outside `spikes/<id>/` and its own logs
347-
348-**Bug fix or design change?** A change is a bug fix only if `PLAN.md`, read literally, already specifies the corrected behavior. Anything `PLAN.md` does not specify is a **plan gap**. The runner then stops and writes `BLOCKED.md`. The Orchestrator either amends `PLAN.md` before any further execution (Class 2 for that spike, logged) or caps the decision at Provisional.
349-
350-**Every run is reported.** Each execution is appended to `runs.log` with the command, the code hash, the seed, and the result. The harness computes the verdict against the pre-registered threshold and prints it machine-readably. Prose never overrides it.
351-
352-**Limits on the runner's role:**
353-
354-- It may file data that supports a challenge (B.9), but it does not change claim state.  
355-- It is not an adjudicator of the packet whose spike it runs.  
356-- It receives `PLAN.md`, not the adjudication reasoning.
357-
358:### 12.3 Deposits and transcripts: when the Orchestrator may write on another agent's behalf
359-
360-**Deliverable deposits.** These are allowed only when the producing agent's sandbox prevents it from writing.
361-
362-- The raw output stays unmodified in `logs/`.  
363-- The deposited file comes from a pre-declared mechanical extraction rule, for example "from the first line matching the required heading to the end of the final message". It is never produced by a model retyping, cleaning up, or summarizing.  
364-- Hashes of both the raw output and the deposited file go into the seal log.  
365-- If the depositor must stay blind to the content (§11.3), the deposit runs as a script, and the content never enters the depositor's context.
366-
367-**Transcripts.** The Orchestrator may deposit any agent's raw transcript into `logs/` at any time.
368-
369-- Transcripts are evidence, not deliverables. A transcript never substitutes for a missing deliverable.  
370-- Nobody else's deliverable may be reconstructed from a transcript, except by the mechanical extraction rule above.
371-
372:### 12.4 Logs and evidence
373-
374-- Use one file per process attempt (for example `<step>-<role>-attempt<N>`). Never truncate or reuse one. Keep failed attempts.  
375-- Evidence cited by an issue or a decision must exist at the cited path when the run ends.
376-
377----
378-
379-## 13. Long-horizon programs
380-
381-A **program** is a sequence of runs, and each run contains one or more packets. Programs fail differently from contests. Decisions get paraphrased across briefs. Provisional results get stacked on each other. Locks get retired while their dependents stay in place. Wall clocks cut steps in half.
382-
383-### 13.1 Unit hierarchy
384-
385-| Unit | Owns |
386-|---|---|
387-| Program | One ledger |
388-| Run | One brief, one pinned method version, one progress file |
389-| Packet | One decision |
390-
391-### 13.2 Program ledger
392-
393-The ledger is an append-only record at program level. Its entries have stable IDs:
394-
395-- **Decisions** (`D-…`): status (Locked / Provisional / Rejected / Superseded); method version; run id; decision-file hash; `depends_on` (list of IDs); spike status.  
396-- **Claims** (`C-…`): grade; state (Candidate / Locked / Challenged / Retired); premises; scope; falsifier; scope-narrowing count (B.9); history.  
397-- **External conclusions adopted as context** (`X-…`), such as prior reviews or literature: source, what it asserts, who adopted it.
398-
399-Entries are never edited. A change is a new entry that `supersedes` the old one.
400-
401-### 13.3 Inheritance across packets
402-
403-- A new brief names the ledger snapshot it inherits (a hash or the last entry ID) and cites inherited items **by ID**.  
404-- Restating an inherited item in prose without its ID is not allowed, because paraphrase is where drift enters.  
405-- How each inherited item binds the new packet:
406-
407-| Inherited item | Binds as |
408-|---|---|
409-| Locked decision or Locked claim | A constraint |
410-| Candidate claim | Guidance only, with no veto |
411-| Provisional decision | An explicit assumption, listed in the brief as such |
412-
413-- **Provisional cap.** A packet may depend on Provisional decisions only to depth 1. It may not build on a Provisional decision that itself depends on another Provisional decision. Raising the cap requires the human lead's signature in the brief.  
414-- Inherited decisions keep the method version they were made under. A MAJOR method bump appends a "re-review due" entry for each affected item. Nothing is re-adjudicated automatically.
415-
416-### 13.4 Dependency propagation
417-
418-When an entry becomes Challenged (admitted, B.9), Superseded, or Retired:
419-
420-- Every entry whose `depends_on` includes it gets an **at-risk** entry in the ledger.  
421-- No new packet may build on an at-risk item until it is re-confirmed or re-decided.
422-
423-### 13.5 Pause, resume, and wall-clock limits
424-
--
518----
519-
520-## Appendix B — Principle necessity protocol
521-
522-This appendix is about **judging claims by necessity**, not by benchmark score. The goal is to extract constraints that can **rule routes in or out before the fact**—closer to physical laws and mathematical theorems—rather than justifying a technique only because some implementation performed well.
523-
524-It does not replace the main orchestration cycle (§4) or Appendix A (model roster). It adds a **Principle track** that can veto purely phenomenological justifications.
525-
526:### B.1 Two different kinds of judgment
527-
528-| | **Phenomenological / performance judgment** | **Principle / necessity judgment** |
529-|---|---|---|
530-| Evidence | Scores, demos, correlations from a specific implementation | Constraints, invariants, impossibilities, obligatory functional roles |
531-| Force | “This can work” (sufficiency) | “That path is blocked / any solution must …” (necessity) |
532-| Analogy | Trying many machines; one is fastest | Thermodynamics: perpetual motion need not be tried again |
533-| Output | Engineering baseline | Transferable theorem-like conclusions |
534-
535-Multi-agent research on open scientific programs should **pursue both**, but must not let the left column quietly promote itself into the right.
536-
537:### B.2 Three grades of necessity (do not conflate)
538-
539-#### Grade 1 — Hard necessity (theorem-like)
540-
541-Under stated assumptions, prove **impossibility** or **obligation**:
542-
543-- Information: without information gain, states cannot be distinguished (sensing lower bounds)
544-- Control: unstable plants cannot be arbitrarily stabilized under given delay/bandwidth limits
545-- Computation: a class of maps is unrealizable under given resource bounds
546-- Embodiment: without force/contact channels, some manipulation classes are impossible
547-
548-**Use:** routes that violate a locked Grade-1 claim may be rejected **without** further bake-offs.
549-
550-#### Grade 2 — Structural necessity (obligatory functional roles)
551-
552-Implementation need not be unique, but certain **functional roles** must appear for a competence class, for example:
553-
554-- State estimation (explicit or implicit)
555-- Error-driven corrective closed loops
556-- Multiple timescales (fast reflexive versus slower intentional)
557-- Treating body dynamics as part of prediction, not as decorative outputs
558-
559-Neuroscience and convergent evolution often supply **candidates** for Grade 2 (recurring functional decomposition), not a unique wiring diagram.
560-
561-**Use:** a proposal must state how each locked role is realized, and name an operational test that would show the role is absent (B.9). Missing roles mark the design **incomplete** in principle—not “maybe it will emerge, so lock it as the mainline.” A role claimed to be realized “implicitly”, with no such test, counts as missing.
562-
563-#### Grade 3 — Path contingency (historical fact, not law)
564-
565-“Earth life happened to take this path” is **not** “every intelligence must.” Specific cortical maps, receptor layouts, or phylogenetic accidents are often Grade 3.
566-
567-**Use:** strong inspiration and existence proofs (the path is feasible). **Do not** write them as the only admissible road unless upgraded to Grade 1 or 2 (missing it is impossible, or the functional role is obligatory).
568-
569-**Core research action for agents:** try to **upgrade** literature and anatomy from Grade 3 → 2 → 1. If the upgrade fails, keep the claim labeled as inspiration and **forbid theorem-style use**.
570-
571:### B.3 What a valid necessity claim looks like
572-
573-A principle claim eligible for locking must have:
574-
575-1. **Explicit premises** (organism/machine class, environment, task, resources)
576-2. A conclusion of only these shapes: impossibility; lower bound (“any solution must…”); functional completeness (roles R1…Rk); invariant (conserved or monotonic relation)
577-3. **Implementation independence** (applies across model families and classical methods alike)
578-4. A clear **violation / falsification mode** (a counterexample downgrades the claim or adds premises)
579-5. **Ban:** benchmark wins alone must never prove necessity—scores support sufficiency only
580-
581-Special cautions:
582-
583-- **Evolution:** lawful use is feasible-region shrinkage under constraints; unlawful use is “mammals did X, therefore every robot must copy X.”
584-- **Neural structure:** lawful use is candidate Grade-2 roles (for example, fast/slow pathways → multi-timescale loops); unlawful use is renaming a brain area as a mandatory network module.
585-
586-### B.4 Lifecycle: Candidate / Locked / Challenged
587-
588-A necessity claim is not fixed at the start, and it is not endlessly fluid. Each claim is in exactly one of three states:
589-
590-| State | May | Must not |
591-|---|---|---|
592-| **Candidate** | Guide exploration and shape proposals | Veto the mainline |
593-| **Locked** | Veto routes justified only by phenomenology. An implementation obeys the claim or files a challenge | Depart from the claim without a challenge packet |
594-| **Challenged** | Stay on record. Once the challenge is *admitted* (B.9), pause the old lock’s veto until adjudication or until the challenge’s deadline | Apply that lock as a veto while an admitted challenge is open |
595-
596-Path for one claim:
597-
598-propose → grade (1, 2, or 3) and scope → **Candidate** → proof or other non-score evidence → **Locked** → counterexample or a change of premises → **Challenged** → upgrade, downgrade, narrow the scope, or retire.
599-
600-Rules:
601-
602-- Phenomenology scores never promote **Candidate → Locked** and never rewrite a **Locked** claim. A score may only open a challenge packet.
603-- A narrow Grade-1 formal bound may lock early, subject to the verification and signature requirements in B.9.
604-- Grade 2 often stays **Candidate**, then locks as the role converges.
605-- Grade 3 stays inspiration unless it is upgraded. It does not enter **Locked** on its own.
606-
607-### B.5 Principle track versus phenomenology track
608-
609-| Track | Output | Authority |
610-|---|---|---|
611-| **Principle track** | Premise → necessity claim → grade (1/2/3) → scope of validity | May **veto** lines of work justified only by phenomena |
612-| **Phenomenology / baseline track** | Performance, demos, correlations | Proves sufficiency only; cannot alone promote a claim to necessity |
613-| **Implementation track** | Algorithms and systems | Must declare which locked principles it obeys; if it violates one, challenge the principle first—do not silently ignore it |
614-
615-Priority when judging a viewpoint:
616-
617-1. Conflicts with a locked Grade-1 claim → reject (no need to try)
618-2. Missing a locked Grade-2 role → mark incomplete (exploration allowed; not eligible as the locked mainline)
619-3. Performance advantage with no principle support → baseline only; not “essentially correct”
620-4. Proposal to upgrade a principle → proof or counterexample, not leaderboard climbing
621-
622-### B.6 Honest scope
623-
--
650-   - *Grade 2:* an argument that *k* realizes a functional role obligatory for the competence class of *k+1*, with an operational test (B.9).  
651-3. **Planning order is not evidence.** The human lead may adopt a ladder as a planning order. That is a brief constraint, scoped to the run or program. It binds the work but is not evidence, and a proposal may not cite the ladder's order as a necessity argument.  
652-4. **Passing a gate is sufficiency only.** A stage gate (a passed spike) is sufficiency evidence about that stage. It never establishes the necessity of the next edge and never changes a grade.  
653-5. **Skipping is allowed unless locked.** Skipping or reordering stages is allowed unless a Locked edge forbids it. A proposal that skips must list the edges it bypasses and the grade of each.  
654-6. **Three different orders.** Evolutionary order, developmental order, and engineering order are distinct. Evidence about one is at most Grade-3 evidence about the others.  
655-7. **Citation-ablation test.** For any argument that cites evolution, development, or anatomy, delete the citation and re-read the argument. If it still stands on its stated premises, the citation was inspiration, which is allowed. If it collapses, the argument is Grade 3 and may not be used theorem-style: not in claims, gates, or vetoes.  
656-8. **Convergence can nominate, not lock.** Convergence across independent lineages under a shared constraint may nominate a Grade-2 candidate more strongly than a single lineage can. It still needs the operational test, and it still locks only under B.9.
657-
658:### B.9 Claim governance
659-
660-1. **Separate roles.** A claim author proposes a claim record (premises, conclusion shape, grade, scope, falsifier). A **claim adjudicator**, who is not the claim author, decides state transitions. With heterogeneous models they are different models, and for high-stakes claims, different families.  
661-2. **Locking requires verification and signature.**  
662-   - **Grade 1:** a proof checked independently of its author. Acceptable checks: a formal checker; an established published result whose premises are matched item by item to the claim's premises; or review by a qualified human. The human lead must also sign. A Grade-1 lock prunes routes with no contest, so it is an irreversible action in the sense of §6 rule 4.  
663-   - **Grade 2:** an argument from premises that applies across implementation classes; a defined operational realization test (rule 3); a logged, serious attempt to find or build a system that reaches the competence class without the role, which failed (`upgrade-log.md`, `violations.md`); and the human lead's signature.  
664-3. **Operational realization test.** A proposal claiming to realize a Grade-2 role must name a test that would show the role is absent. Examples: a probe that must recover the estimated quantity within a stated error; an intervention on the named signal that must produce a stated effect; a timescale separation measurable in the closed-loop response. Without such a test, the role is not realized.  
665-4. **Challenge admissibility.** A challenge pauses a lock's veto only once it is *admitted*. Admission requires either (a) a concrete counterexample, with artifacts, that satisfies the claim's stated premises, or (b) evidence that a premise fails in the target setting. The claim adjudicator rules on admission and sets a deadline. If the challenge is unresolved by the deadline, the human lead decides whether the veto resumes. A challenge that is not admitted is filed and pauses nothing.  
666-5. **Gates before ranking.** Appendix B gates apply before rubric ranking (§4 Step 4). A rubric row about necessity discipline may score the quality of reasoning, but it never substitutes for the gate.  
667-6. **Scope-narrowing counter.** Log every narrowing of a claim's scope, and every premise added so the claim can survive a counterexample. At the third such event (the brief may set a different default), the claim goes to mandatory review for retirement. A claim that survives only by shrinking is degenerating.  
668-7. **Researcher agreement is not grade evidence.** Several researcher model families agreeing that a claim holds is evidence about how persuasive the argument is. It is not evidence of implementation independence (B.3.3), and it moves no grade.  
669-8. **Counterexample searches are experiments.** They run under §12.2, with a `PLAN.md` stating what counts as a counterexample. The runner files the data and does not change claim state.
670-
671----
